-- title:
--   Feasible nodes and coherent branches
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Let $f_k(v,\cdot)$ be detector values on a witness set $B$.
--
--   1. **Prefix detection.** A witness $d$ detects the prefix of $v$ (level $n$) if
--   $$
--   f_i(\mathrm{anc}_i(v),d)\ge\tfrac12\quad\text{for all }0<i\le n .
--   $$
--   2. **Feasible nodes.** Nodes with some detecting witness in $B$. They form a finite set at each level, and feasibility passes to ancestors.
--   3. **Branches.** A choice of one node per level with each earlier node an ancestor of the later ones. By König's lemma, if every level has a feasible node, there is a branch all of whose nodes are feasible.
